General elections were held in the [[Serbia and Montenegro|Yugoslav]] province of [[Republic of Serbia (1992–2006)|Serbia]] on 21 September 1997, to elect the [[President of Serbia|president]] and members of the [[National Assembly (Serbia)|National Assembly]]. With no presidential candidate receiving over 50% of the vote in the first round, a second round was held on 5 October.<ref name=BI>[http://www.balkaninsight.com/en/article/serbian-presidential-elections-since-1990 Serbian Presidential Elections Since 1990] Balkan Insight, 1 April 2012</ref> Running on a platform of [[Serbian nationalism|nationalism]] and [[neoliberalism|neoliberal economic reforms]], [[Vojislav Šešelj]] of the [[Serbian Radical Party]] received the most votes in the runoff. However, [[voter turnout]] was only 49%, below the required 50%.<ref name=BI/><ref>{{Cite book |last=Rosenstone |first=Steven J. |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1xp3vfx |title=Forecasting Presidential Elections |date=1983 |publisher=Yale University Press |isbn=978-0-300-02691-7 |pages=1-8}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=CNN.com - World - Election Watch |url=http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/election.watch/europe/serbia.prez.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20010506181654/http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/election.watch/europe/serbia.prez.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=May 6, 2001 |access-date=2024-03-15 |website=CNN}}</ref> As a result, the elections were annulled, and [[1997 Serbian presidential election|fresh elections]] were scheduled for December.<ref name=BI/><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 January 1998 |title=REPUBLIC OF SERBIA: RERUN OF THE PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION DECEMBER 7 AND DECEMBER 21, 1997 |url=https://www.osce.org/files/f/documents/a/4/15133.pdf |website=osce.org |publisher=[[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe|OSCE]]}}</ref>

In the National Assembly elections, the [[Socialist Party of Serbia]]–[[Yugoslav Left]]–[[Liberals of Serbia|New Democracy]] coalition emerged as the largest in the Assembly, winning 110 of the 250 seats.<ref>Janusz Bugajski (2002) ''Political Parties of Eastern Europe: A Guide to Politics in the Post-Communist Era'', pp434</ref>

The elections were [[Election boycott|boycotted]] by several major opposition parties, including the [[Democratic Party (Serbia)|Democratic Party]], the [[New Democratic Party of Serbia|Democratic Party of Serbia]] and the [[Civic Alliance of Serbia]], which claimed that the elections would not be held under fair conditions.<ref name=BI/><ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-01-08 |title=MILOSEVIC'S PARTY CLAIMS VICTORY IN SERBIAN VOTE |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1997/09/22/milosevics-party-claims-victory-in-serbian-vote/7f5f3ffa-1f41-4676-8d25-ba439e492b56/ |access-date=2024-03-15 |work=Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> Most [[Kosovo Albanians]] also boycotted the elections, who made up around 17% of the population, due to increasing ethnic tensions in [[Kosovo]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=CNN - Many boycott Serbia ballot - Sept. 21, 1997 |url=http://www.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/21/serbia.election.update/ |access-date=2024-03-15 |website=www.cnn.com}}</ref>

